  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191213/assets/8dfbfa4aaa55e2f8175fe829fccc945d29733a84fc5c1641f099b09d805ada2b.png)\n\nSince the angles $∠BOD$ and $∠DOA$ are supplementary, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠DOA \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{134}^{∘} \\\\  & ={46}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD & =\\frac{m∠BOD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{46}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={23}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA & =m∠COD+m∠DOA \\\\  & ={23}^{∘}+{134}^{∘} \\\\  & ={157}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
